---
// How Kyle builds with AI coding agents, plus the development principles every project
// follows. The principles come from github.com/kyle-pace/principles at build time
// (src/lib/principles.ts), so this page always matches what the agents read.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getPrinciples, REPO_URL } from '../lib/principles';

export const prerender = true;

const { html: principles } = await getPrinciples();
---

<Base
  title="How I build"
  description="How a product manager ships apps and sites with AI coding agents, and the principles every project follows."
  image="/og-how-i-build.png"
  imageAlt="How I build: a product manager who ships software, with AI agents and a shared set of principles."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">How I build</p>
      <h1>A product manager who ships software.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I’m Director of Product at <a href="https://www.localpublic.tv/">Local Public</a>. On nights and weekends I
        build apps and sites with AI coding agents, including Henwood, Seattle Grocery Deals and the experiments on this
        site. I don’t write the code by hand. This page is how I make that work, and the principles every project
        follows.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>Not quite a developer, not just a PM</h2>
    <p>
      I came to tech from restaurants, by way of a UX bootcamp, then project management, then product. I’m not a
      developer by training. But I really am building apps now, and that’s an odd place to be.
    </p>
    <p>
      The way I make sense of it: I run my projects the way I’d run a product team. Coding agents moved the hard part.
      Typing code used to be the scarce skill. Now it’s deciding what to build, writing a clear brief, setting
      constraints, judging what comes back and saying “no, not like that.” That’s work I already do every day.
    </p>

    <h2>How a project runs</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Decide what it’s for.</strong> Every project starts with a short brief: who it’s for, what it should do
        and what it shouldn’t. Plans and decisions live in Confluence, so they outlast any one conversation with an agent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Give it its own repo.</strong> One project, one repo, one deploy. Small pieces are easier to reason
        about, and easier to throw away.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Set the rules once.</strong> The principles below live in <a href={REPO_URL}>a GitHub repo</a>{' '}
        and sync into every project as an <code>AGENTS.md</code> file, the shared
        format most coding agents read. Whichever agent I use, it starts from the same standards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Brief outcomes, not code.</strong> I describe what I want and the constraints around it. The agent does
        the building.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Review everything.</strong> AI doesn’t build a track record with me. Every session starts fresh, and it
        sounds just as sure of itself when it’s wrong. So I don’t delegate the way I would to a teammate. I review every
        result, ask it to explain choices I don’t follow, and try things myself before they ship. I’m less of a manager
        and more of an editor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Let checks catch what I miss.</strong> Every build has to pass. An automated check fails it if a page
        loads anything from a server I haven’t approved, and the agents run an accessibility check on every change.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ship small.</strong> Pushing to main deploys to Cloudflare. Changes go out in small pieces, so I can
        actually check them.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What I’m still learning</h2>
    <p>
      The gap isn’t syntax. It’s judging the output, because when I direct agents, I’m the only reviewer. So I keep
      working on understanding code well enough to judge it, and on data, which matters most for my day job right now.
    </p>

    <h2 id="principles">The principles</h2>
    <p>
      These are written for the agents, which is why they talk about me in the third person. They’re pulled from <a href={REPO_URL}>github.com/kyle-pace/principles</a>{' '}
      each time this site builds, so this page always matches
      what the agents see.
    </p>
    <div class="principles" set:html={principles} />
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  article {
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .steps li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .principles {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-8);
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .principles :global(h3) {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .principles :global(h3:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .principles {
      padding: var(--space-4);
    }
  }
</style>
